zh=[('本章導讀','分鏡、預視覺化與鏡頭清單將戲劇意圖轉為可討論、排程及拍攝的視覺計畫。分鏡呈現畫面與動作；previs 測試時間、空間與技術條件；鏡頭清單記錄拍攝單位與需求。它們是協作工具，不是不可更改的命令。[R101][R102]'),('學習目標與適用對象','讀者將學會選擇視覺規劃媒介、拆解戲劇節拍、建立鏡頭清單、比較方案對觀眾資訊和資源的影響，並檢查 AI 提案的連續性與可行性。適用導演、攝影、分鏡、美術、剪輯、製片及學習者。'),('先備知識','建議具備場景目標、景別、視點、軸線、場面調度及剪接連續性概念（CH03、CH12、CH13）。不必會畫圖；簡筆圖、平面圖、照片板和文字卡均可溝通。'),('核心術語','分鏡（storyboard）：連續圖格表達構圖、動作及資訊變化的溝通文件。預視覺化（previsualization/previs）：拍攝前測試影像和動作。技術預視覺化（techvis）：檢視攝影機、場地、器材和可行性。鏡頭清單（shot list）：可更新的鏡號、內容、技術需求及狀態紀錄。動態分鏡（animatic）：分鏡圖配暫定時間與剪接的粗剪。[R102][R103]'),('歷史與基本原理','電影製作使用素描、平面圖、文字拆解、攝影草圖和剪接測試溝通畫面，並無各產業通用的單一順序。數位工具方便測試和共享，但不能取代敘事判斷。分鏡讓團隊檢視資訊、節奏、空間和資源，不保證逐格拍攝。[R101]'),('傳統製作方法','導演與編劇先定場景目的、人物目標、轉折及觀眾所需資訊；分鏡師或導演畫關鍵節拍並註明景別、運動、動作、視線和聲音。攝影、美術、特效、剪輯和製片依限制審閱。簡單場景可用縮圖；複雜動作可製作 animatic、3D previs 或 techvis。訪談案例顯示分鏡是思考和溝通工具，現場可調整。[R104][R105]'),('AI 時代的技術轉換與限制','生成模型可產生構圖提案、整理描述或建立鏡頭表；3D／即時引擎可檢視空間和攝影機路徑。[R102][R103] 生成圖不能保證跨格人物、道具和時間連續，也無法證明實景安全。輸出標為提案，由人類審查；避免輸入未授權劇本、個資或機密。'),('詳細工作流程','1. 寫場景意圖及觀眾資訊目標。2. 標節拍、轉折、必要動作。3. 列限制、連戲風險和覆蓋需求。4. 畫關鍵格，避免逐句台詞切鏡。5. 標人物、視線、攝影機、方向和聲音。6. 用 animatic 檢查時間。7. 複雜場景做低細節 previs／techvis。8. 鏡頭表列鏡號、場景、景別、運動、內容、設備、特效、風險、負責人、狀態、版本。9. 跨部門確認。10. 現場依安全、表演、天候、預算修訂並留原因。[R102][R106]'),('提示詞與技術參數','提示詞指定場景目的、人物位置、動作前後、景別、畫幅、視線、運動、連戲條件、排除項和格式。示例：「六格黑白線稿，原創虛構兩人走廊對話；服裝、門窗、180 度軸線一致；逐格列鏡號、景別、動作、視線、運動和節拍目的；不模仿電影畫面或商標；附可編輯 JSON。」參數只記錄實測工具與版本；不虛構 seed 或 API 欄位。'),('電影案例分析','《三塊廣告牌》（Three Billboards Outside Ebbing, Missouri）：攝影指導 Ben Davis 說導演 McDonagh 事前畫粗略分鏡；拍攝時未必逐格參照，但團隊已有共同理解，顯示分鏡可作溝通而非命令。[R104]《魔宮奇兵》（Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom）：Spielberg 訪談談以分鏡溝通及剪輯中評估鏡頭資訊量；這是個別案例，不是通用格數規範。[R105] 合法觀看片源版次與時間碼待補。'),('常見錯誤與品質檢查','錯誤：逐句切鏡、缺少戲劇目的、鏡號不一、視線或道具跳變、不可行運鏡、將 previs 當安全審查、AI 偷換角色空間。檢查：逐格寫目的；縮圖看節奏；平面圖核軸線；校驗鏡號和版本；請攝影、製片、特效、安全人員審查；保留修改理由。[R102][R103]'),('練習、評量與延伸研究','用自寫 30–60 秒雙人場景，列四節拍，設計 A 少鏡維持空間、B 以反應鏡頭改變資訊兩套方案。提交 6–10 格分鏡、短 animatic、鏡頭清單及修訂紀錄。評量目的清晰、資訊可讀、空間連續、可行性和交接完整；創意須人工評閱。延伸比較 2D、animatic、3D previs 的溝通成本。'),('參考資料','來源以出版社、製作人員訪談、專業協會及官方軟體文件為主。出版社摘要不等同全書；製作文章不代替合法觀片和時間碼紀錄。參見 R101–R106。')]
heads=['Chapter Overview','Learning Objectives and Audience','Prerequisites','Core Terms','History and Basic Principles','Traditional Production Practice','Technical Shifts and Limits in the AI Era','Detailed Workflow','Prompt Template and Technical Parameters','Film Case Studies','Common Errors and Quality Checks','Exercises, Assessment, and Further Research','References']
en=['Storyboards, previs, and shot lists translate dramatic intent into visual plans. Boards show images and action; previs tests timing, space, and technical conditions; shot lists record planned units and needs. They support collaboration, not fixed commands. [R101][R102]','Readers learn to select a visualization method, break beats into shots, prepare a shot list, compare effects on audience information and resources, and review AI proposals for continuity and feasibility. For production learners and practitioners.','Familiarity with scene goals, shot scale, point of view, axis, blocking, and continuity is useful (CH03, CH12, CH13). Drawing is optional; sketches, plans, photo boards, and text cards work.','Storyboard: sequential panels communicating framing, action, and information. Previsualization/previs: pre-shoot testing of image and action. Techvis: planning camera, location, equipment, and feasibility. Shot list: updateable IDs, content, needs, status. Animatic: rough timed edit of panels. [R102][R103]','Film teams use sketches, plans, written breakdowns, camera drawings, and edit tests; no universal workflow exists. Digital tools help test and share plans but do not replace narrative judgment. Boards help inspect information, rhythm, space, and resources; they do not guarantee every panel will be shot. [R101]','The director and writer define purpose, goals, turns, and audience information. Artists draw key beats and annotate scale, movement, action, eyelines, and sound. Departments review constraints. Thumbnails may suffice; complex scenes may need animatics, 3D previs, or techvis. Interviews show boards can aid communication without binding the set. [R104][R105]','Models can draft compositions, organize descriptions, suggest shots, or structure tables; 3D tools help inspect space and camera paths. [R102][R103] Generated images cannot guarantee continuity or prove safety. Mark them proposals for human review. Do not submit unlicensed scripts, personal data, or confidential material.','1. State scene intention and audience goal. 2. Mark beats and actions. 3. List constraints, continuity risks, coverage. 4. Draw key panels, not every line. 5. Annotate people, eyelines, camera, direction, sound. 6. Use an animatic to test timing. 7. Use previs/techvis for complex space. 8. Record IDs, scene, scale, movement, content, gear, VFX, risk, owner, status, version. 9. Confirm across departments. 10. Revise for safety, performance, weather, budget; log why. [R102][R106]','Specify purpose, positions, action, camera, aspect ratio, eyelines, movement, continuity locks, exclusions, and output. Example: “Six-panel monochrome storyboard for an original corridor conversation; keep wardrobe, doors, and 180-degree axis consistent; label ID, scale, action, eyeline, movement, beat purpose; do not imitate a film or logo; include editable JSON.” Log tested tool/version parameters only.','**Three Billboards Outside Ebbing, Missouri**: cinematographer Ben Davis said McDonagh drew rough boards; they were not necessarily followed on set, but built shared understanding. Boards communicate rather than command. [R104] **Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom**: Spielberg’s ASC interview discusses boards for communication and assessing shot information in editing. This specific case is not a universal panel-count rule. Lawful edition and timecodes remain to be logged. [R105]','Errors include mechanical dialogue coverage, unclear purpose, mismatched IDs, continuity breaks, impossible moves, substituting previs for safety review, and AI changing scene details. Check purpose, rhythm, axis, IDs, versions, feasibility with department leads, and revision reasons. [R102][R103]','Create two versions of an original 30–60 second dialogue scene: fewer shots with stable space, and reactions that shift audience information. Submit 6–10 panels, short animatic, shot list, revision log. Assess clarity, continuity, feasibility, handoff. Human review judges creativity. Compare costs of 2D, animatic, and 3D previs.','Sources prioritize publishers, practitioner interviews, professional societies, and official documentation. Publisher summaries are not full books; articles do not replace lawful viewing or edition/timecode logs. See R101–R106.']
assert len(zh)==len(en)==13
